00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:07.580
A 2022 Model 3 with LFP chemistry was checked for capacity loss following 78,000 miles of use. The test targets assumptions about rapid degradation in lithium iron phosphate packs under real world conditions.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:24.080
Results are positioned to inform owner expectations around long term battery health in daily driving. The vehicle in question is a standard Model 3 built with the LFP pack chemistry that Tesla has used in certain markets for cost and longevity reasons.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:30.720
Capacity retention at that mileage gives an early data point on how these packs behave outside lab conditions.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:50.880
Battery longevity data from real world LFP packs is beginning to separate marketing claims from measurable economics. When a 2022 Model 3 retains usable capacity after 78,000 miles, the cost per mile of ownership drops because replacement cycles stretch further into the vehicle service life.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:05:05.960
That shift changes how buyers calculate total cost of ownership and how insurers price long-term coverage. Fleet operators gain an additional lever when they can project higher utilization before packs reach retirement thresholds.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:23.120
The same data set also pressures the narrative that all lithium chemistries degrade at similar rates. LFP different degradation curve means fleet operators can plan for higher utilization before packs reach the typical 70 to 80% retirement threshold.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:41.380
Manufacturers that lock in LFP for high mileage duty cycles therefore face lower warranty reserves and slower inventory churn for replacement modules. A residential unit failed completely after one year of service cutting off both solar export control and emergency power.

